// ================= ABSTRACT =================
push(H1("Abstract"));
push(rich([
 T("Quantitative structure-activity relationship (QSAR) studies on small congeneric series frequently apply supervised univariate descriptor filtering before cross-validation. This inflates apparent performance, a leakage established in principle yet common in practice, whose interaction with the correlation structure of large descriptor pools is rarely quantified. Using minimum inhibitory concentrations of 31 monoterpenoids against five pneumonia-associated bacterial strains, determined by liquid broth microdilution, we compared apparent and fully nested leave-one-out validation across three routes that vary the order of unsupervised correlation grouping and supervised effect-size filtering over 2,208 cleaned descriptors, alongside a leakage-free reference set of 13 literature-derived descriptors. Significance was assessed by whole-pipeline label permutation, 2,000 per model, with descriptor selection repeated in every fold. Apparent cross-validated Q\u00b2 collapsed under nesting, for "),
 I("Staphylococcus aureus"), T(" from 0.51 to 0.10, and only "), I("S. aureus"), T(" and "), I("Streptococcus pneumoniae"),
 T(" retained a significant positive nested Q\u00b2 under every route. Under permutation, correlated descriptors passed the supervised filter in families, so the number selected exceeded a binomial independence expectation by roughly threefold when grouping preceded filtering and tenfold when it followed. Choosing one representative per correlation group by network centrality, a fully label-free criterion, reduced this inflation to about 1.7-fold and made the selected-descriptor count interpretable, isolating a genuine excess for the two responsive strains. Endpoints with few actives, such as "),
 I("Pseudomonas aeruginosa"), T(" with three, produced degenerate null distributions and were treated as artefacts. Descriptor filtering remains legitimate for a final interpretable model fitted on all data; only the performance estimate requires nesting. At n = 31 the reported associations are hypothesis-generating.")
]));
push(new Paragraph({ spacing:{after:60}, children:[new TextRun({text:"Scientific contribution", bold:true, font:FONT, size:22})]}));
push(body("We show that in correlated descriptor spaces nested validation alone does not guarantee an honest significance test, because supervised filters select descriptors in correlated families and inflate the permutation null far beyond binomial expectation. We introduce a label-free, centrality-based choice of group representative that restores the interpretability of that null and isolates genuine signal, and we quantify the apparent-to-nested collapse across four selection routes. This distinguishes the work from earlier demonstrations of selection bias, which address leakage in the point estimate but not the dependency-driven distortion of the null distribution used for inference."));
push(new Paragraph({ spacing:{after:120}, children:[
  new TextRun({text:"Keywords: ", bold:true, font:FONT, size:22}),
  new TextRun({text:"molecular descriptors; QSAR; feature selection; small sample size; n:p ratio; nested cross-validation; selection bias; permutation testing; PLS-DA; monoterpenoids", font:FONT, size:22})]}));

// ================= INTRODUCTION =================
push(H1("Introduction"));
push(body("Quantitative structure-activity relationship (QSAR) modelling predicts biological activity from molecular structure and is an established tool in early drug discovery and natural-product research [1, 2]. Its central premise is that activity is encoded in quantifiable molecular features whose relationship to a measured endpoint can be captured by a statistical model. In practice, a handful of classical physicochemical parameters is sometimes not sufficient to explain why some closely related compounds are active while others are not. In such cases it becomes necessary to capture the relevant structural and electronic information more comprehensively, which usually requires large, computationally generated descriptor sets. This is the origin of the statistical difficulty addressed here."));
push(body("Modern platforms generate thousands of descriptors from structure alone, whereas activity-driven studies, in particular natural-product discovery and early medicinal chemistry, are limited to tens of compounds by synthesis, isolation and assay costs. The number of descriptors (p) then greatly exceeds the number of samples (n), which is the small-n, large-p problem, also known as the curse of dimensionality. In this regime the multivariate methods most used in QSAR can produce apparent separation of random data. Ruiz-Perez and colleagues showed with synthetic data that partial least squares discriminant analysis (PLS-DA) is reliable only around a sample-to-feature ratio of roughly 2:1 [3]. Controlling the n:p ratio is therefore a prerequisite for any trustworthy small-sample model, not a cosmetic refinement."));
push(body("Two descriptor-selection strategies recur in the literature. The first computes a very large descriptor pool from one or more libraries and reduces it, typically by a univariate statistical criterion such as a rank-sum test with an effect-size threshold [4], combined with the removal of highly intercorrelated descriptors [5, 6], and sometimes followed by re-selection of descriptors with a high variable importance in projection (VIP). The VIP threshold conventionally used for that re-selection, VIP greater than 1, is itself dependent on the number of features entering the model and should be raised when that number is large [4]. The second pre-selects a small number of chemically interpretable descriptors on mechanistic grounds and reduces residual redundancy by correlation only. Both are widely used, but their statistical validity in the small-n regime is rarely examined critically."));
push(body("A distinction that is well established in principle, yet still routinely disregarded in practice, separates the two. Correlation filtering between descriptors is unsupervised, because it never uses the activity labels, and is therefore free of information leakage. A univariate effect-size filter, or a VIP-based re-selection, is supervised: it uses the labels. When such a supervised selection is performed on the whole dataset before cross-validation, the left-out samples have already influenced which descriptors are retained, so the cross-validated error and any permutation test that keeps the selection fixed are optimistically biased [7, 8, 9]. When p greatly exceeds n this bias is severe, because some descriptors always separate a given small dataset by chance [10, 11]. An honest estimate requires that the entire supervised selection be repeated inside every cross-validation fold, which is nested validation, and that permutation tests randomise the labels through the complete pipeline; the combination of double cross-validation with permutation testing has been advocated for exactly this purpose in PLS-DA models of omics data [12, 13]."));
push(body("What is far less well characterised is how this interacts with the correlation structure of a large descriptor pool. Nesting corrects the point estimate, but the reference against which that estimate is judged, the permutation null distribution, is itself distorted when descriptors are strongly intercorrelated. A supervised filter applied under random labels does not select descriptors independently but in correlated families, so the number passing it, and the apparent performance attainable from them, exceed what an independence-based expectation predicts. A nested procedure can therefore still be compared against a null that is too permissive, and the count of surviving descriptors, often reported as though it measured how many features genuinely discriminate, becomes uninterpretable. We are not aware of this dependency inflation having been quantified for QSAR descriptor pools, nor of a remedy that removes it without itself using the activity labels."));
push(rich([T("Here we address both points, using a previously published dataset of 31 monoterpenoids tested against five pneumonia-associated bacterial strains [14] as a vehicle rather than as the object of study. We first establish, for these data, how much of the apparent performance of a supervised effect-size filter survives fully nested validation, and which endpoints survive it under every route we examine. We then quantify the dependency inflation of the permutation null and show that it depends strongly on the order of the two reduction steps, ranging from about tenfold the binomial expectation when the filter is applied first to about threefold when the unsupervised grouping precedes it. Finally we propose and evaluate a label-free remedy: choosing the representative of each correlation group by network centrality rather than by effect size removes the last supervised element from the grouping step, reduces the inflation to about 1.7-fold and makes the number of selected descriptors interpretable, which in turn isolates a genuine excess of discriminating descriptors for two of the five endpoints. A small set of literature-derived descriptors, reduced only by unsupervised correlation grouping, serves throughout as a leakage-free reference against which the data-driven routes can be judged. The chemical findings are hypothesis-generating and are reported as such; the contribution we claim is methodological.")]));

// ================= MATERIALS AND METHODS =================
push(H1("Materials and methods"));
push(H2("Data set"));
push(rich([T("The workflow was demonstrated on a previously published data set of 31 monoterpenoids tested against five pneumonia-associated bacterial strains ("),
 I("Haemophilus influenzae"), T(" ATCC 49247, "), I("Pseudomonas aeruginosa"), T(" ATCC 27853, "),
 I("Staphylococcus aureus"), T(" ATCC 29213, "), I("Streptococcus pneumoniae"), T(" ATCC 49619 and "),
 I("Streptococcus pyogenes"), T(" ATCC 19615) [14]. Antibacterial activity in the liquid (broth) phase of the broth microdilution volatilization assay was the endpoint. For each strain, compounds were assigned a binary class (active versus non-active) from their mean minimum inhibitory concentration, giving five independent classification endpoints. The active/non-active split was 10/21 ("),
 I("H. influenzae"), T("), 3/28 ("), I("P. aeruginosa"), T("), 5/26 ("), I("S. aureus"), T("), 6/25 ("),
 I("S. pneumoniae"), T(") and 11/20 ("), I("S. pyogenes"), T("). "), I("P. aeruginosa"),
 T(", with only three actives, was treated as descriptive only and excluded from predictive claims.")]));

push(H2("The data set and its limitations"));
push(rich([T("This data set was chosen deliberately for its imperfections, not despite them. With only 31 compounds it is small, and the active/inactive split is imbalanced and endpoint-specific, ranging from a relatively balanced 10/21 and 11/20 ("),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(", "), I("S. pyogenes"), T(") to 5/26 and 6/25 ("), I("S. aureus"), T(", "), I("S. pneumoniae"),
 T(") and an extreme 3/28 for "), I("P. aeruginosa"),
 T(", which has too few actives for predictive modelling and is reported as descriptive only. The monoterpenoids form a narrow, structurally homogeneous chemical class, which supports mechanistic interpretation within the class but limits descriptor variance and external applicability. Activity was obtained by dichotomising minimum inhibitory concentrations from the standardised broth microdilution volatilisation assay against defined ATCC reference strains. The assay is reproducible, but dichotomisation discards information and makes the labels threshold-dependent, and borderline compounds can influence the small active class disproportionately. No external test set is available, so all estimates are internal. The analysis started from 3,507 computed descriptors, of which 2,208 remained after cleaning; for 31 compounds this corresponds to sample-to-feature ratios of roughly 1:113 before and 1:71 after cleaning, both far from the ratio of about 2:1 regarded as adequate for PLS-DA [3].")]));
push(body("These properties are exactly why the data set is instructive here. The aim of this study is to expose the problems and difficulties of descriptor selection followed by PLS-DA in the small-n, large-p regime. A small, imbalanced and chemically narrow set makes selection bias, overfitting and the instability of supervised selection visible in a way that a large, balanced, chemically diverse and near-ideal data set would not. A near-perfect data set would have produced cleaner models but would have been far less suited to the critical, methodological discussion that is the purpose of this work. Accordingly, all results are framed as associations within this data set rather than as validated predictors of new compounds."));

push(H2("Route A: large multi-library descriptor pool"));
push(H3("Stage 1: descriptor generation"));
push(body("Canonical SMILES from PubChem [15] were used to compute descriptors on the ChemDes platform [16] from five libraries: Chemopy [17], PaDEL [18], RDKit [19], BlueDesc [20] and Pybel [21], which together yield on the order of 3,500 descriptors. Non-numerical, missing-value and autocorrelation descriptors were removed, together with exact duplicates and constant or near-zero-variance descriptors. Typically about 2,000 to 2,500 descriptors remain after this step; the exact pool size for the present data set is given in the Case study section."));
push(H3("Stage 2: correlation-based feature grouping (unsupervised)"));
push(body("Spearman (rho) and Pearson (r) coefficients were computed between all pairs of the descriptors retained after Stage 1, and a pair was treated as redundant when the absolute value of either coefficient exceeded 0.7 at p < 0.05. Redundant descriptors were consolidated into feature groups by an iterative most-connected-first procedure adapted from Comesana and colleagues [5], and uncorrelated descriptors were retained individually. Stages 1 and 2 together are a substantial reduction: only about 5 % of the originally computed descriptors survive as group representatives, the remainder being either removed as uninformative during cleaning or absorbed into a correlated group. The resulting number of groups for the present data set is given in the Case study section. The grouping criterion uses no activity labels, so it is identical for all five endpoints and needs to be computed only once. Within each group a single representative was retained; two rules for choosing that representative were compared and are described in Stage 3."));
push(H3("Stage 3: supervised effect-size filtering"));
push(body("Each descriptor was tested for its ability to discriminate active from non-active compounds with the Wilcoxon rank-sum test (Mann-Whitney U). Throughout this work the effect size of a descriptor denotes the rank-biserial correlation derived from that test (equivalently r = Z divided by the square root of N), interpreted using the conventional thresholds of Cohen [22], for which values of about 0.1, 0.3 and 0.5 correspond to small, medium and large effects. With this definition in place, the choice of group representative in Stage 2 was made in one of two ways. In the main analysis, denoted Route A1, the representative is the group member with the largest effect size, which makes this sub-step label-dependent. In a fully label-free variant, denoted Route A1u, the representative is instead the group member with the highest centrality, defined as the largest mean absolute correlation with the other members of its group; the entire grouping step is then independent of the activity data. Representatives with p not greater than 0.05 and an absolute effect size above 0.3 were retained. This filter is again strongly reductive: it removes roughly 75 % of the remaining representatives in Route A1 and roughly 90 % in Route A1u, the difference reflecting that a representative chosen by centrality is not pre-selected for a large effect size. The resulting descriptor counts per endpoint for Route A1 and Route A1u are given in the Case study section. The p-values were not adjusted for multiple testing, so no Benjamini-Hochberg or comparable correction was applied. This step uses the activity labels and is therefore supervised, which is why it has to be repeated inside every cross-validation fold."));
push(H3("Stage 4: final selection and n:p constraint"));
push(body("The retained descriptors form the strain-specific set used for modelling. Where a fixed model size was required, the descriptors with the highest effect size were retained so as to keep the sample-to-feature ratio near 2:1, that is p not greater than n/2, which is about 15 for n = 31 [3]."));
push(H3("Alternative order of the two reduction steps"));
push(body("The same two reduction steps can also be applied in the opposite order. In Route A1, used above, the descriptors are grouped first and the effect-size filter is applied to the group representatives. In the reversed arrangement, denoted Route A2, the supervised effect-size filter is applied first to the whole cleaned pool, and the correlation grouping is then applied to the survivors; the resulting descriptor counts for the present data set are given in the Case study section. Because the grouping then operates on a label-selected subset, it differs between endpoints and has to be recomputed inside every cross-validation fold, whereas in Route A1 the grouping is identical for all endpoints and needs to be computed only once. Route A1 and Route A2 were evaluated with identical nested validation so that the influence of this design choice could be quantified; both are reported throughout the results below."));

push(H2("Route B: small, literature-derived interpretable descriptors"));
push(body("Independently, 25 interpretable two-dimensional descriptors were selected a priori from the monoterpenoid structure-activity literature. They span constitution (molecular weight, carbon and oxygen counts, heavy-atom and ring counts), saturation and shape (fraction of sp3 carbon, aliphatic and aromatic rings, C=C count, stereocentres, degree of unsaturation), functional groups (hydrogen-bond donors and acceptors, and SMARTS indicators for aldehyde, ketone, carboxylic acid, phenol, ether, ester or lactone and aliphatic alcohol) and physicochemistry (Crippen logP and molar refractivity [23], Ertl TPSA [24], Labute ASA). All descriptors were computed with RDKit [19] from neutral, canonical SMILES, without 3D optimisation or pH and protonation correction. Rotatable bonds used the strict definition, aromaticity the RDKit default model, and the count of stereocentres included unspecified centres, that is atoms that are potential stereocentres but carry no assigned configuration in the input structure. Exact definitions and SMARTS patterns are given in Additional file 1. Because this set is fixed a priori from external knowledge, it uses no activity information and is leakage-free by construction. Residual redundancy was removed by unsupervised correlation grouping with the same threshold as in Route A, with rare binary indicators (fewer than four positive compounds) kept as singletons because their correlations are unstable at n = 31. This removed roughly 50 % of the a priori set; the resulting number of retained descriptors is given in the Case study section."));

push(H2("PLS-DA modelling and validation"));
push(body("Descriptor sets were modelled by PLS-DA with autoscaling and leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV). Autoscaling here means that every descriptor was mean-centred and divided by its standard deviation, so that each contributes unit variance and descriptors of large numerical range do not dominate the latent variable. The centring and scaling parameters were computed from the training compounds of each fold only and then applied to the held-out compound, never from the complete data set, since parameters estimated on all compounds would carry information about the held-out one. Every model reported in this work uses a single latent variable. This is the conservative choice in the small-n regime: it cannot be tuned to the data, and it avoids the optimism that arises when the number of components is chosen on the same data used to report the result. Two-component projections appear only in the illustrative score plots of the Additional file. Model quality is reported as the cross-validated Q\u00b2 and the area under the ROC curve (AUC), together with sensitivity, specificity, balanced accuracy and the Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC), all obtained from the leave-one-out predictions at a decision threshold of 0.5. Q\u00b2 and AUC are threshold-free and are used for the significance testing; the confusion-matrix metrics are reported alongside them because they describe what a model does in practice, namely how many active compounds it recovers (sensitivity) and how many inactive compounds it correctly leaves aside (specificity). The Matthews correlation coefficient is a single-number summary of the two-by-two confusion matrix that ranges from -1 to +1, with 0 corresponding to chance; unlike plain accuracy it remains informative when the classes are strongly imbalanced, as may well be the case for many small activity data sets, where a model that predicts every compound to be inactive would already reach a high accuracy. Statistical significance was assessed by a permutation test with 2,000 random relabelings, with p calculated as one plus the number of permuted values at least as extreme as the observed value, divided by one plus the number of permutations."));
push(body("The type of permutation test was matched to the selection step. For the supervised Route A, the effect-size filter and the feature grouping were repeated inside every LOOCV fold using the training data only, and the 2,000 permutations randomised the labels through the complete pipeline, which is fully nested validation with whole-pipeline label scrambling. For the unsupervised Route B, a simple permutation with the fixed descriptor set is already honest, because no supervised selection precedes the model; apparent and cross-validated performance therefore largely coincide. The two-stage VIP procedure was evaluated in a naive variant and in a nested variant to quantify its bias, as described in the corresponding results section. Multiplicity should be addressed by a Bonferroni threshold according to the number of statistically evaluable endpoints."));
push(body("Concretely, each permutation randomised only the class-label vector, while the descriptor matrix was left unchanged. For every permuted labelling the entire Route A pipeline was recomputed from scratch inside each leave-one-out fold and on the training partition only. This comprised, in whichever order applies to the route being tested, the correlation grouping and the rank-biserial effect size with its p-value, together with the ranking used for any fixed model size and the PLS-DA fit. For Route A1 and Route A1u the correlation groups themselves are label-independent and were therefore computed once outside the permutation loop, while the choice of group representative, the effect-size filter and everything downstream were recomputed for every permutation and every fold. For Route A2 the effect-size filter is the first step and was recomputed for every permutation and every fold, and the subsequent grouping of the label-selected survivors was recomputed with it. This ensures that the advantage of choosing the best-separating descriptors from a large pool is itself contained in the null distribution, which an ordinary permutation that holds the selected descriptors fixed does not achieve."));

push(H2("Software"));
push(body("Route A descriptor calculation used the ChemDes platform [16] with the Chemopy [17], PaDEL [18], RDKit [19], BlueDesc [20] and Pybel [21] libraries. The correlation analysis and the illustrative PLS-DA score plots used MetaboAnalyst 5.0 [25]. Route B descriptors, the correlation-based reduction, all cross-validated and nested PLS-DA models, the permutation tests and the VIP calculations were implemented in Python using RDKit [19], scikit-learn [26], SciPy [27], NumPy [28] and pandas [29]. All analysis scripts and data tables are provided as Additional files to ensure full reproducibility."));
push(H3("Use of large language models"));
push(body("A large language model (Claude Opus 5.0; Anthropic, PBC) was used, under the direction and supervision of the authors, to assist with implementing the statistical-analysis code, in particular the nested cross-validation and whole-pipeline permutation procedures, with generating figures, and with drafting and editing the manuscript text. It was not used to generate or interpret the underlying biological activity data. All code, analyses, results and interpretations were reviewed and verified by the authors, who take full responsibility for the content. In accordance with COPE (Committee on Publication Ethics) and Springer Nature policy, the model does not meet the criteria for authorship and is not listed as an author."));

// ================= CASE STUDY =================
push(H1("Case study: antimicrobial monoterpenoids"));
push(body("To test the two descriptor-selection routes and the two orders of the Route A reduction steps under a realistic small-n, large-p scenario, we applied all of them to the 31-monoterpenoid data set. Route A started from 3,507 descriptors computed by five ChemDes libraries, reduced to 2,208 after cleaning, which for 31 compounds is an initial ratio of about 1:71. In Route A1 the unsupervised correlation grouping reduced this pool to 182 representatives, the same for every endpoint, and the subsequent effect-size filter left strain-specific sets of 35 to 56 descriptors; in Route A2 the effect-size filter was applied first, leaving 373 to 603 descriptors, and the subsequent grouping left 27 to 50 representatives (Fig. 1a, Table 1a). Route B used the 25 pre-selected descriptors reduced to 13 by unsupervised correlation grouping (Fig. 1b). The VIP-based selection, in which a first PLS-DA ranks the Route A1 descriptors and a second PLS-DA is fitted on the 15 highest-ranked of them, is shown in Fig. 1c and evaluated below."));
push(img("/mnt/user-data/outputs/Fig1_two_route_scheme.png", 580, 477, "png"));
push(legend("1","Descriptor-selection routes evaluated in this study; full details are given in Materials and methods. (a) Route A reduces a large multi-library pool by an unsupervised correlation grouping and a supervised effect-size filter, applied either in that order (Route A1) or in the reverse order (Route A2); in the variant Route A1u the group representative is chosen by centrality rather than by effect size, which makes the grouping entirely label-free. (b) Route B uses a small set of interpretable descriptors chosen a priori and reduced only by correlation grouping. (c) The VIP-based selection ranks the Route A1 descriptors in a first PLS-DA and reports a second PLS-DA fitted on the 15 highest-ranked descriptors. Orange marks steps that use the activity labels and are therefore a source of selection leakage, green marks label-independent steps, blue marks PLS-DA models and grey marks data steps without descriptor selection."));

