push(tblCap("Table 1a|  Cross-validated predictive ability (Q2) of Route A1, Route A2 and Route B. Rows give the descriptor counts and the successive analyses; columns give the five strains. Apparent denotes supervised selection performed once on all compounds, nested denotes selection repeated inside every leave-one-out fold with whole-pipeline permutation, and Route B denotes the 13 literature-derived descriptors after unsupervised reduction. All Route A models use one latent variable, so apparent and nested rows differ only in where the descriptor selection is performed. Route B is given both at one latent variable, for direct comparability with Route A, and at three, which is the setting an inner cross-validation selects for this route (Table S8); because Route B contains no supervised selection, both are honest estimates and neither requires nesting. Permutation p-values are in parentheses and are reported as <0.001 where no permutation reached the observed value. For the nested rows an asterisk marks p < 0.05 and n.s. a non-significant result; the apparent rows carry no significance marker, because that p-value comes from the circular procedure this study argues against and is shown only for numerical contrast. The dagger marks P. aeruginosa, which has three actives and is descriptive only. One entry requires comment: under Route B with three latent variables, S. pyogenes reaches p = 0.048 while its Q2 is -0.01. The two statements are compatible but answer different questions. The permutation p-value asks whether the procedure performs better with the true labels than with random ones, and the null distribution of a three-component model at n = 31 lies well below zero, so a value near zero can fall in its upper tail. The Q2 itself asks whether the model predicts better than simply assigning every compound the class mean, and a negative value means it does not. Such a result should therefore not be read as a useful model, and we do not count S. pyogenes as a positive finding anywhere in this work."));
