push(legend("2b","Fully nested permutation test of Route A1 for all five strains, based on 2,000 permutations of the entire pipeline. Histograms show the null distribution of Q2 (left) and AUC (right); the observed value (solid line) is compared with the 95th percentile of the null distribution (dashed line). The observed values fall inside the null distribution for H. influenzae and S. pyogenes, which are therefore not significant, and exceed it only weakly for S. aureus and S. pneumoniae."));

push(H2("Route B: a leakage-free interpretable set recovers a genuine signal"));
push(rich([T("The 25 literature-derived descriptors, reduced only by unsupervised correlation grouping to 13, gave an honestly significant, moderate signal for "),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(", "), I("S. aureus"), T(" and "), I("S. pneumoniae"),
 T(" even with a single latent variable, with Q\u00b2 values of 0.29, 0.39 and 0.35, AUC values of 0.82 to 0.85 and Q\u00b2 permutation p-values of 0.002, 0.001 and 0.002 (Fig. 2a, Table 1a, Table 1b). Because the reduction uses no labels, the simple permutation test is already honest and apparent and cross-validated performance largely coincide. These are precisely the strains that Route A lost or weakened under nesting. Route B also tolerates a larger model than Route A: with three latent variables, the setting that an inner cross-validation selects for this route, the honest Q\u00b2 rises to 0.46, 0.42 and 0.44 for the same three strains, all at p < 0.005 (Table 1a, Table 1b). We report both settings because the one-component model is directly comparable with Route A while the three-component model is the better description of what this descriptor set can do; the reasons for the difference between the routes are set out below. Under the Bonferroni threshold of alpha = 0.0125 for the four statistically evaluable endpoints, "),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(" and "), I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" remained significant and "), I("S. aureus"),
 T(" was borderline, with p = 0.017 for the AUC.")]));
push(rich([I("S. pyogenes"),
 T(" remained weak with this reduced set (Q\u00b2 = 0.06, significant for Q\u00b2 at p = 0.035 but not for AUC at p = 0.127) even though it has the largest active class of all five endpoints, with 11 of 31 compounds active. This observation deserves emphasis because it separates two explanations that are easily confused. If the weak result were driven by low statistical power, that is by too few active compounds, then "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(" should have been the most favourable endpoint rather than the least favourable one. Instead the three endpoints with fewer actives ("),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(" with 10, "), I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" with 6 and "), I("S. aureus"),
 T(" with 5) all yielded clearly significant models with the same descriptors and the same validation procedure. The weakness for "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(" is therefore endpoint-specific rather than sample-size-driven.")]));

push(rich([T("Which descriptors carry the discrimination is not the same for every organism, and the fixed literature set makes this visible without any selection step intervening. The VIP curves of the 13 Route B descriptors (Fig. S3a) show that "),
 I("S. aureus"), T(" and "), I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" are driven by nearly the same descriptors, whereas "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(" departs clearly from them and "), I("H. influenzae"),
 T(" occupies an intermediate position. Because this descriptor set is fixed a priori and identical for every strain, the difference is a property of the endpoints and not of the pipeline. It can also be quantified: the Spearman rank correlation between the VIP profiles is 0.88 for "),
 I("S. aureus"), T(" against "), I("S. pneumoniae"), T(", by far the highest of all pairs, while "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(" reaches only 0.14 to 0.29 against the other three and is thus the clear outlier; "),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(" lies between these extremes at 0.43 and 0.65. The two endpoints that remain significant under every route are therefore also the two whose descriptor profiles agree most closely, which is a further internal consistency and suggests a shared structural basis for activity against these two organisms.")]));

push(H2("Does the correlation reduction of Route B help or harm?"));
push(body("The correlation grouping that reduces the 25 a priori descriptors to 13 is leakage-free but not automatically beneficial: it discards descriptors that are correlated with a retained representative, and correlation with one descriptor does not mean redundancy for every endpoint. PLS-DA, moreover, is comparatively tolerant of correlated predictors, because it condenses them into latent variables rather than estimating a separate coefficient for each. We therefore repeated the Route B analysis using all 25 descriptors, with the same single latent variable and the same 2,000 permutations. Since no supervised selection is involved, the two variants are directly comparable (Table 2)."));
push(rich([T("The comparison is instructive because it goes in both directions. For three of the four statistically evaluable endpoints the reduced 13-descriptor set was the better model, with a higher Q\u00b2 for "),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(" (0.29 against 0.14), "), I("S. aureus"), T(" (0.39 against 0.31) and "), I("S. pneumoniae"),
 T(" (0.35 against 0.32); here the removal of redundant descriptors evidently removed noise. For "), I("S. pyogenes"),
 T(" the opposite held, and by a wide margin: the full 25-descriptor set raised Q\u00b2 from 0.06 to 0.24 and AUC from 0.62 to 0.84, and the result became clearly significant on both metrics (p = 0.004 and 0.002). This refines the interpretation given above. It is not that the a priori descriptor set fails to capture activity against "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T("; rather, the correlation reduction removed descriptors that were redundant for the other endpoints but carried endpoint-specific information for "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(". Under the Bonferroni threshold for four evaluable endpoints, alpha = 0.0125, the 25-descriptor model for "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(" remains significant.")]));
push(body("Two cautions apply to this observation. First, it was found by testing a second, larger descriptor set after the first had been analysed, so the comparison is exploratory and the improvement for one endpoint out of four should not be treated as a confirmed result. Second, it does not license using the larger set everywhere, since for the other three endpoints the reduced set was better. The practical lesson is narrower and, we think, more useful: an unsupervised reduction is safe with respect to leakage, but it is still a modelling choice with a cost, and reporting both variants is inexpensive here."));
