push(H2("The two-stage VIP procedure reproduces the bias"));
push(body("A procedure that is sometimes used in the literature is to fit a first PLS-DA model, to keep only the descriptors with a high VIP, and to fit a second model on this reduced set. Only the second model is usually reported. We reproduced this procedure explicitly in order to quantify its bias, and we describe it here in full because the details determine whether it is legitimate. The procedure is summarised in Fig. 1c."));
push(body("The entry point is the Route A1 descriptor set, that is the 35 to 56 descriptors that survive the correlation grouping and the subsequent effect-size filter for a given strain. A first PLS-DA with one latent variable was fitted on this set and VIP scores were computed from it; this model serves only to rank the descriptors and its own performance is not reported. The 15 descriptors with the highest VIP were retained, and a second PLS-DA, again with one latent variable, was fitted on those 15 descriptors and is the model whose performance is reported. Using a fixed number of 15 descriptors rather than the conventional threshold of VIP above 1 keeps the model size identical to the effect-size-selected top-15 model, so that the two selection criteria can be compared directly; it also avoids the conventional VIP > 1 threshold, whose meaning is unclear here for the reasons given in the Discussion."));
push(body("The critical distinction is where the VIP selection is performed. In the naive form, which corresponds to the procedure as it is usually described, the first model and hence the VIP ranking are computed once on all 31 compounds, and only the second model is cross-validated. In the nested form all three steps are repeated inside every fold. The naive form gave a much higher cross-validated Q2 for every endpoint: 0.42 against 0.02 for H. influenzae, 0.50 against -0.01 for S. aureus, 0.57 against 0.08 for S. pneumoniae and 0.51 against 0.14 for S. pyogenes (Table 4). The gap between the two forms is the leakage already described for the effect-size filter, now produced by the VIP ranking instead. Per-strain VIP values are given in Additional file 3 (20260710_VIP_EffectSize_Tabellen.xlsx) and Fig. S2."));

push(H2("Does the n:p-constrained (top-15) selection rescue Route A?"));
push(rich([T("Route A retains the 15 descriptors with the highest effect size per strain when a fixed model size is required, so as to keep the sample-to-feature ratio near 2:1. We evaluated this top-15 model with the same honest machinery (Table 3, Fig. 3a, Fig. 3b). Constraining the model to 15 descriptors did not restore the apparent performance under nesting. For "),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(" the nested Q\u00b2 was indistinguishable from zero (0.005, p = 0.068) and for "), I("S. aureus"),
 T(" it was -0.02 (p = 0.074), so both endpoints that were weak or negative with the full set remained so. More tellingly, the significance pattern shifted relative to the full descriptor sets. "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(" now became significant, with a nested Q\u00b2 of 0.18 (p = 0.015), while "), I("S. aureus"),
 T(" lost the significance it had with the full set, although the underlying data were identical. This instability, in which the set of apparently significant endpoints depends on the exact size of the supervised selection, is itself a symptom of the small-n regime. Keeping the n:p ratio near 2:1 is necessary but not sufficient, because the supervised selection remains label-dependent and must still be validated by nesting. "),
 I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" remained significant under both model sizes, and "), I("P. aeruginosa"), T(" remained an artefact. Restricting Route A2 to its 15 highest-effect-size descriptors gave the same qualitative picture (Fig. 3a): the nested Q\u00b2 for "),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(" and "), I("S. aureus"), T(" remained non-significant (-0.16 and -0.04), while "), I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" and "), I("S. pyogenes"), T(" were significant (0.14 and 0.17, p = 0.012 and 0.015). Both orderings of Route A therefore agree that the n:p-constrained selection does not rescue the full-set result and shifts, rather than stabilises, which endpoints reach significance.")]));
