push(H2("Effect-size versus VIP selection, and what changes with model size"));
push(rich([T("The 15 Route A1 descriptors that enter the n:p-constrained model can be chosen either by effect size (Table 3) or by VIP, the latter through the two-stage procedure described above (Table 4). We compared both criteria on identical data, at identical model size and with identical apparent and fully nested validation, using 2,000 whole-pipeline permutations for each. Apparent performance was comparable and uniformly high under both criteria, with apparent Q\u00b2 between 0.40 and 0.58 across the two criteria and apparent permutation p-values of about 0.001 throughout, that is regardless of what the nested analysis would later show. Under nesting both criteria collapsed in the same way and, at this model size, agreed on which endpoints survive: "),
 I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" and "), I("S. pyogenes"), T(" were significant under both, and "), I("H. influenzae"), T(" and "), I("S. aureus"),
 T(" under neither. The two criteria nevertheless differed considerably in magnitude, for instance a nested Q\u00b2 of 0.16 against 0.08 for "),
 I("S. pneumoniae"), T(", so agreement on the significance verdict should not be mistaken for agreement on the estimate.")]));
push(rich([T("Two conclusions follow, and it is worth separating them carefully. The first is that our data do not show VIP selection to be inherently inferior to effect-size selection. At equal model size the two criteria agreed on every significance verdict, and the nested estimates were of similar magnitude, with VIP slightly better for the two non-significant endpoints and effect size slightly better for the two significant ones (nested Q\u00b2 0.16 against 0.08 for "),
 I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" and 0.18 against 0.14 for "), I("S. pyogenes"),
 T("). What is problematic about VIP-based selection is therefore not the ranking itself but how it is customarily used: computed once on all compounds and outside the cross-validation, it leaks exactly as the effect-size filter does, and the conventional VIP > 1 threshold is not a criterion of validity, since the mean squared VIP equals 1 by construction and the number of descriptors exceeding it depends on how many entered the model [4].")]));
push(rich([T("The second and more consequential instability is not between the two selection criteria but between model sizes. With the full Route A1 descriptor sets the significant endpoints were "),
 I("S. aureus"), T(" and "), I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" (Table 1a); restricting the same pipeline to 15 descriptors makes "),
 I("S. aureus"), T(" non-significant and "), I("S. pyogenes"),
 T(" significant instead, under either selection criterion (Table 3, Table 4). The underlying data are identical and only the size of the supervised selection has changed. That the set of significant endpoints depends on this essentially arbitrary choice, while the apparent performance stays uniformly high and cannot distinguish the cases, shows that supervised selection is unstable at this sample size.")]));
