push(H2("What the models do in practice: sensitivity, specificity and MCC"));
push(body("Q2 and AUC are threshold-free summaries and are the quantities on which the permutation tests are based, but they do not say how a model would behave if it were actually used to triage compounds. We therefore also report the confusion-matrix metrics obtained from the same leave-one-out predictions at a decision threshold of 0.5 (Additional file 5, Table S6). These are informative in a way the Q2 values alone are not, and they temper the more favourable reading of the significant results."));
push(rich([T("Two features stand out. First, specificity is uniformly high and sensitivity is uniformly low, which is the expected behaviour of a model fitted to a strongly imbalanced data set: under nested Route A1 the specificity was 0.71 to 0.96 while the sensitivity ranged from 0.20 to 0.50. For "),
 I("S. aureus"), T(", the endpoint with the smallest active class, the significant nested Route A1 model recovered only one of the five active compounds (sensitivity 0.20, specificity 0.96, balanced accuracy 0.58, MCC 0.24), and under Route A2 two of five (sensitivity 0.40, balanced accuracy 0.66, MCC 0.35). A statistically significant Q2 therefore does not imply a practically useful classifier, and reporting plain accuracy would have been actively misleading here, since a model that called every compound inactive would already reach 26 of 31 correct for this endpoint. Second, the strongest nested Route A model, "),
 I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" under Route A1, reached a balanced accuracy of 0.73 and an MCC of 0.54, and the leakage-free Route B models reached comparable or better values: MCC 0.44 for "),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(", 0.75 for "), I("S. aureus"), T(" and 0.67 for "), I("S. pneumoniae"),
 T(", with sensitivities of 0.70, 0.60 and 0.50 at specificities of 0.76, 1.00 and 1.00. The ordering of the routes by MCC therefore agrees with their ordering by Q2, which is a useful internal consistency check, and it again places the small a priori descriptor set ahead of the large data-driven pool.")]));
push(body("How large an MCC should be considered useful cannot be answered by a universal threshold, and we would caution against importing the rules of thumb sometimes quoted for correlation coefficients. The Matthews coefficient is bounded above by the class distribution: with 5 actives among 31 compounds, a model that recovers three of the five actives without any false positive already reaches an MCC of about 0.75, whereas the same sensitivity in a balanced data set would give a markedly lower value. An MCC must therefore be read against the class ratio and the sample size of the data set that produced it. As a practical orientation for data of the present kind, that is a few dozen compounds with a strongly imbalanced endpoint, we would regard an honestly nested MCC below about 0.3 as indicating no usable classification, values between 0.3 and 0.6 as a signal worth pursuing but not sufficient for triage, and values above 0.6 as a genuinely useful model, while noting that with five actives a single additional misclassification moves the coefficient by more than 0.1 and that the confidence interval around any of these values is correspondingly wide. The MCC of 0.75 obtained for S. aureus under Route B, for instance, rests on three correctly identified active compounds and would fall to about 0.55 if one of them were missed."));

push(H2("How the reduction scheme affects the result and how many descriptors genuinely discriminate"));
push(body("Route A combines a supervised effect-size filter with an unsupervised correlation grouping, and three arrangements of these two steps were evaluated on identical data with identical nested validation (Table 1a, Table 1b, Table 5, Fig. 4). Route A1, used throughout the results above, groups all 2,208 descriptors first and then applies the filter to the 182 representatives, choosing each representative as the group member with the largest effect size. Route A1u differs in one respect only: the representative is chosen as the group member with the highest centrality, so that the grouping becomes completely independent of the activity labels; its full results are given separately in Table 5, since its descriptor counts and Q2/AUC values do not otherwise appear in Table 1a or Table 1b. Route A2 reverses the two steps and applies the filter to all 2,208 descriptors before grouping the survivors."));
push(body("The three schemes differ systematically in how many univariate tests they perform and in how dependent those tests are (Fig. 5a). Route A2 tests all 2,208 descriptors, and because the pool still contains the full redundancy of five overlapping libraries, the standard deviation of the null distribution of the hit count is about ten times the value expected for independent tests. Route A1 tests only 182 representatives, which lowers this inflation to about 3.3-fold, and Route A1u lowers it further to about 1.7-fold. The residual inflation under Route A1 arises because the representative of each group is selected as the member with the largest effect size, which is a best-of-many choice that raises the effect size of the representative even under random labels; removing that choice, as in Route A1u, removes most of the remaining dependence. A convergent observation has been made in untargeted metabolomics, where reducing the feature set before the multivariate step gave fewer but more stable and reproducible discriminating features than selecting them afterwards, and where VIP values were shown to grow with the number of input features [6]. Both findings point the same way as ours: what a supervised statistic reports depends on how many candidates it was allowed to choose from."));
push(body("The consequence for the interpretation of the univariate hit counts is substantial and is shown in Fig. 5. Under Route A2 the observed counts of 373 to 603 lie well inside the range that random labels can produce, so no conclusion about the number of genuinely discriminating descriptors is possible. Under Route A1u the null distribution is narrow and close to the naive expectation of 5 % of 182 tests, and the observed counts can therefore be interpreted: for S. aureus 25 descriptors passed against a null mean of 7.5 (p = 0.012) and for S. pneumoniae 20 against 7.0 (p = 0.020), whereas for H. influenzae and S. pyogenes the observed counts of 11 and 15 were within the null range (p = 0.218 and 0.101). The two endpoints with a genuine excess of univariate hits are exactly the two endpoints whose nested models are significant, which is a reassuring internal consistency."));
push(body("The honest performance estimates differ less than these structural properties. Under nested validation the set of significant endpoints was S. aureus and S. pneumoniae for all three schemes (Table 1a, Table 1b, Table 5), so the central conclusion of this study does not depend on the reduction scheme. Route A1 gave the highest nested Q2 for S. pneumoniae (0.30 against 0.19 for Route A2 and 0.14 for Route A1u), whereas Route A2 gave the highest value for S. aureus (0.16 against 0.10 and 0.08). Route A1u gave systematically lower Q2, which is the price of choosing the representative without reference to the labels, but it also reduced the P. aeruginosa artefact markedly, from a nested Q2 of about 0.40 under the other two schemes to 0.13, because the descriptors are no longer selected in a way that favours separation of the three active compounds."));
push(body("Taken together, we recommend the grouping-first arrangement as the default. Route A1 is preferable when the aim is the best honest predictive estimate, and Route A1u is preferable when the aim is to interpret how many descriptors genuinely discriminate, because only then is the univariate hit count meaningful. Both are simpler to describe and cheaper to compute than Route A2, because the grouping is performed once rather than separately for every endpoint and every fold. Neither arrangement removes the need for nested validation, since the effect-size filter remains supervised in all of them."));
