// ================= RESULTS AND DISCUSSION =================
push(H1("Results and discussion"));
push(H2("Redundancy reduction is unsupervised and belongs first"));
push(body("Large descriptor pools are highly redundant, because constitutional, topological and connectivity indices frequently encode the same property. Collapsing each cluster of descriptors whose absolute Pearson or Spearman coefficient exceeds 0.7 to a single representative, while keeping uncorrelated singletons, removes redundancy without discarding chemically distinct information and is fully auditable. The grouping criterion uses no activity labels, so it is leakage-free, identical for every endpoint and, in principle, need not be repeated inside the cross-validation folds. This is why we place it first and recommend that arrangement: applied to the complete pool it reduces 2,208 descriptors to 182 representatives in a single, transparent step, whereas in the reversed arrangement it operates on a label-selected subset that differs between endpoints and between folds. The one caveat is the choice of representative within a group. If that choice is made by effect size, as in the original procedure, this single sub-step reintroduces a dependence on the labels and the whole sequence must still be nested. If it is made by an unsupervised criterion such as centrality, the grouping becomes entirely label-free and only the subsequent filter requires nesting. Route B is the limiting case in which no supervised selection occurs at all and no nesting is required."));
push(H2("Why both Spearman and Pearson"));
push(body("The Pearson coefficient r captures linear associations, whereas the Spearman coefficient rho captures any monotonic relationship. Using both, so that a descriptor pair counts as redundant if either coefficient exceeds 0.7, is the more conservative choice, and with count and binary descriptors at n = 31 it catches monotonic but non-linear redundancies that Pearson underestimates. In the interpretable Route B set, which contains 25 descriptors and therefore 300 possible descriptor pairs, 30 pairs exceeded the threshold. Of these 30 redundant pairs, 25 were detected by both coefficients, three were detected only by Spearman and two only by Pearson. The clearest Spearman-only case was the pair formed by the hydrogen-bond-donor count and TPSA, with r = 0.57 but rho = 0.72, which reflects a strictly monotonic but stepwise relationship between a small integer count and a continuous surface-area descriptor. The consequence for the final model was minor: the combined criterion reduced the 25 descriptors to 13, whereas a Pearson-only criterion would have retained 14, the difference being one representative swap within the same redundant block. The result is therefore robust to the choice of coefficient."));
push(H2("Supervised filtering outside cross-validation invalidates apparent performance"));
push(body("The central result of this study is a cautionary one. Reducing a very large pool by a supervised effect-size filter and by correlation, and then reporting the cross-validated performance of the resulting model, appears to work, because Route A gave positive apparent Q2 values and permutation p-values of about 0.0005 for four strains. This apparent success is, however, largely an artefact of selection bias. The effect-size filter uses the activity labels of all 31 compounds to choose descriptors, so a left-out compound has already influenced the selection. The ordinary permutation test, which permutes labels only for the modelling step while holding the label-selected descriptors fixed, is therefore circular and reports a spuriously small p-value. When p greatly exceeds n, some descriptors always separate a particular small dataset by chance, so this route effectively selects, and then rewards, stable noise [7, 8, 11]. When the entire selection is moved inside the cross-validation and the whole pipeline is permuted, the apparent ability disappears for H. influenzae and S. pyogenes, whose nested Q2 values are negative. In short, the widely used route that combines a large pool with an effect-size filter yields statistically invalid performance estimates when it is validated in the usual, non-nested way. It must be checked by nested validation before any predictive claim is made."));

push(body("Selection is not the only step that can leak, and it is worth stating how the possible sources compare in size. Two further ones arise from preprocessing. Autoscaling parameters are frequently estimated once on the complete data set, and descriptor ranks for a univariate filter are frequently computed once as well, on the grounds that ranks do not depend on the class labels. Both shortcuts let the held-out compound influence the values used to predict it. We avoided them: throughout this work the centring and scaling parameters and the ranks entering the effect-size filter are recomputed inside every fold from the training compounds alone. To gauge what that costs and what it is worth, we repeated the whole analysis with the autoscaling parameters estimated globally instead. Had we scaled in that more convenient way, the nested Q2 would have come out higher by 0.014 on average and by up to 0.043 for an individual endpoint. The values we report are therefore free of that particular optimism rather than affected by it. Set against the 0.42 by which the nested Q2 falls when the descriptor selection is moved outside the folds, the preprocessing step matters roughly thirty times less. Two conclusions follow. Global scaling is a real but minor source of optimism, which is reassuring for the many published analyses that use it, and the problem addressed in this work lies unambiguously in the supervised selection rather than in the preprocessing that surrounds it."));
push(rich([T("The ordering of these sources also extends upwards. The supervised step examined throughout this work is a filter, which merely applies a threshold; wrapper methods instead search actively for the subset that performs best, and a genetic algorithm coupled to a cross-validated fitness function is the most widely used example [30]. We implemented such an algorithm on our data, using the same population size, crossover and mutation rates as that work and searching the 182 label-free representatives rather than the full pool, since the latter spans about 10^665 subsets. Run once on all compounds, the algorithm reported a cross-validated Q\u00b2 of 0.64 for "),
 I("H. influenzae"), T(" and 0.72 for "), I("S. aureus"), T(". Repeated inside every fold, the honest values were -0.59 and -0.08 (Table S12). The collapse of 1.23 and 0.80 is two to three times the 0.42 produced by our filter, and it is largest for "),
 I("S. aureus"), T(", the one endpoint where our filter finds a reproducible signal. The freedom to search is therefore not a neutral design choice but the dominant determinant of how far a selection procedure inflates its own estimate, and a wrapper of this kind is defensible only where an external test set exists, as in the study cited, and not where performance is read off the same data that guided the search.")]));

push(rich([T("A related diagnostic deserves comment, because it is widely used and would have given the wrong answer here. The gap between the apparent fit R\u00b2 and the cross-validated Q\u00b2 is a recognised indicator of overfitting, and a difference below roughly 0.3 is often taken as reassurance. For the Route A1 models that difference is 0.09 to 0.13, so all of them would pass comfortably, yet the same models lose between 0.26 and 0.62 of their Q\u00b2 once the selection is repeated inside every fold (Table S11). For "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(" a gap of 0.11 accompanies a nested Q\u00b2 of -0.13. The comparison with Route B shows why: there the gaps are larger, 0.19 to 0.40, although those are the honestly predictive models. The criterion is therefore not merely insensitive to selection leakage but inverted by it, because R\u00b2 and Q\u00b2 are both computed on descriptors already chosen with the same labels, so their difference reflects only the modelling step while the leakage sits in the selection step preceding both. A small R\u00b2 minus Q\u00b2 is consequently not an argument against overfitting whenever any label-dependent step precedes cross-validation.")]));

push(H2("When can the number of univariate hits be interpreted?"));
push(body("A natural objection to the pessimistic reading above is that far more descriptors pass the effect-size filter than would be expected by chance, so that the surplus ought to reflect genuine discrimination. Whether that argument is valid turns out to depend entirely on how the reduction is arranged, and the three schemes compared here separate the question cleanly (Fig. 5, Fig. S6)."));
push(body("When the filter is applied to all 2,208 descriptors, as in Route A2, the argument fails. The naive expectation of 5 % of 2,208, which is about 110 descriptors, assumes 2,208 independent tests, and the data violate that assumption severely. Permuting the class labels 1,000 times and recomputing the filter yields a null distribution with a mean of about 90 but a standard deviation of about 95, which is roughly ten times the binomial value of 10.2, with individual permutations reaching 611 to 826 hits. A single fortuitous label split is echoed by whole families of correlated descriptors that pass together. The observed counts of 373 to 603 therefore lie inside the range that chance alone produces; for S. aureus the null distribution reaches 692 against 603 observed. One cannot subtract 110 and treat the remainder as real, nor identify which specific descriptors are the true positives. The full null distributions for all three schemes are shown in Fig. S6."));
push(body("When the descriptors are grouped before they are tested, the picture changes (Fig. 5a). Route A1 tests 182 representatives instead of 2,208 descriptors and the inflation falls to about 3.3-fold, and Route A1u, in which the representative is chosen without reference to the labels, lowers it further to about 1.7-fold, with a null mean of about 7.3 against the naive expectation of 9.1. At that point the count becomes interpretable (Fig. 5b). For S. aureus 25 of the 182 representatives passed the filter against a null mean of 7.5 (p = 0.012), and for S. pneumoniae 20 passed against 7.0 (p = 0.020), so these two endpoints show a genuine excess of univariate association that chance does not explain and lie outside the 2.5 to 97.5th percentile range of the null distribution. For H. influenzae and S. pyogenes the observed counts of 11 and 15 were within the null range (p = 0.218 and 0.101) and no such claim can be made. The two endpoints with a significant excess of hits are precisely the two whose nested models are significant, which is a consistency between two largely independent lines of evidence."));
push(body("The practical lesson is therefore not that counting univariate hits is meaningless, but that it is only meaningful once the tests have been made approximately independent and the selection of what is tested is itself free of the labels. It should be added that these p-values were not corrected for multiple testing, so a modest surplus is expected even under weak signal, and that a genuine excess of univariate association still says nothing about whether a multivariate model built from those descriptors will generalise. That question is answered only by the whole-pipeline permutation test."));
push(body("The same caution applies to visual evidence. Two-dimensional PLS-DA score plots of the selected descriptors show convincing separation of active and inactive compounds for every strain (Fig. S5), including the strains that fail nested validation. Apparent separation on the training data, whether summarised as a high apparent Q2, a small ordinary permutation p-value or a clean score plot, is a property of fitting a flexible model to few samples in many dimensions and is not evidence of predictive ability."));
push(H2("Yet nested validation can still succeed and the procedure is not worthless"));
push(body("Nesting does not simply destroy all signal. Despite only 31 compounds, several of them barely active, the fully nested analysis remained significant for S. aureus and S. pneumoniae, with Q2 values of 0.16 and 0.19 and p-values not greater than 0.013. The supervised route can therefore be meaningful for some datasets, provided that the performance is estimated by nested validation rather than by the biased non-nested procedure. The lesson is not to abandon supervised selection, but to validate it honestly: filtering is permitted, but the performance estimate must be nested."));
push(H2("A small, interpretable, unsupervised-reduced set is the statistically cleaner route"));
push(body("Route B avoids the problem at its source. Because the descriptors are fixed a priori from mechanistic knowledge and the only data-driven step, the correlation grouping, is unsupervised, there is no label leakage to correct, and a simple permutation test is already honest. This route recovered a genuine, moderate and interpretable signal for three strains with a single latent variable, and its apparent and cross-validated performance agree. A pre-selected set of this kind therefore carries real, if modest, predictive value, and its descriptors can be interpreted mechanistically without the circularity that afflicts data-driven selection. The trade-off is that a priori selection is subjective, but subjectivity that is transparent and label-independent is preferable, in the small-n regime, to an objective-looking yet leaky data-driven filter."));
push(H2("Disadvantages of two-stage VIP-based selection"));
push(body("Selecting descriptors by VIP in a first model and re-using only the high-VIP descriptors in a second model is attractive but has two drawbacks. First, VIP is a model-internal quantity [31, 32]: the mean squared VIP equals 1 by construction, so some descriptors exceed 1 in every model, including a model fitted to noise. A VIP above 1 is therefore a ranking heuristic within an already-validated model and not independent evidence of predictive value. Second, and more seriously, VIP is computed from the labels, so selecting on it outside the cross-validation is the same leakage as with any supervised filter. Our comparison of the naive and nested variants shows that the resulting Q2 is heavily inflated, for example 0.42 against 0.02 for H. influenzae and 0.57 against 0.08 for S. pneumoniae (Table 4). We therefore recommend using VIP only for interpretation within a validated model. If it is used for selection, that selection must be nested. With a well-chosen a priori set the second VIP stage is usually unnecessary, because the reduced set already models honestly."));
push(H2("Effect of class balance"));
push(body("Because activity was defined by the minimum inhibitory concentration, the active/non-active split is not balanced and ranges from 3/28 to 11/20. At this scale, moderate imbalance affects statistical power only marginally, and the dominant constraint remains the n:p ratio. A more subtle point is that the supervised effect-size filter becomes less stable as the active class shrinks. The two best-balanced endpoints retained the smallest sets, with 37 descriptors for H. influenzae (10 actives) and 35 for S. pyogenes (11 actives), whereas the imbalanced endpoints retained more, with 56 for S. aureus (5 actives), 48 for S. pneumoniae (6 actives) and 52 for P. aeruginosa (3 actives). The relationship is not strictly monotonic, since P. aeruginosa, which has the fewest actives, did not retain the largest set, and the same non-monotonicity is visible in the raw hit counts of Fig. 5, where S. pyogenes produces many hits despite having the most actives. Class imbalance is therefore one contributing factor among several rather than a single explanatory variable, and the pattern is best read as a symptom of instability rather than of stronger discrimination. It reinforces the need for nested validation."));

// ================= CONCLUSIONS =================

push(H2("Better data give better Q2 values"));
push(body("The honest performance ceiling reached here is a property of this data set, not of the method. To show this, we applied Route A1, Route A1u and Route A2 unchanged to a published, independent data set of a very different kind: the SELDI-TOF mass spectra of serum from 20 healthy controls and 19 patients with Gaucher disease reported by Smit and colleagues [33], comprising 590 m/z variables per sample. The pipeline, the single latent variable, the leave-one-out cross-validation and the 2,000 whole-pipeline permutations were exactly as described in Materials and methods; only the input matrix was exchanged. All three routes gave nested Q2 values between 0.38 and 0.45 at p < 0.001, with nested AUC between 0.87 and 0.89 and a balanced accuracy of about 0.80 (Table S7). These values are substantially higher than any obtained for the monoterpenoids, where the best nested Q2 was 0.30 and only two of four evaluable endpoints reached significance at all."));
push(body("The reason is not a better method but better data. The Gaucher data set has a sample-to-variable ratio of about 1:15 rather than 1:71, its classes are almost balanced at 19 against 20 rather than 3 to 11 actives out of 31, and its endpoint is a diagnosed disease state rather than a threshold applied to a continuous minimum inhibitory concentration. The same nesting machinery that removes most of the apparent performance in the monoterpenoid data therefore leaves a strong and reproducible signal here, while still showing the characteristic optimism of the apparent estimate, which reaches Q2 = 0.71 to 0.76 and an apparent AUC of 0.99. This is the clearest possible demonstration that our conclusions concern the combination of a small, imbalanced data set with supervised selection, and not nested validation as such: given adequate data, the identical procedure yields a strong honest result. The same data also allow the detection limit of the pipeline to be estimated directly, by thinning them repeatedly to the class sizes of our own endpoints and rerunning the analysis unchanged (Table S10). With ten or eleven cases the Gaucher signal survives in essentially every random draw, so the negative results for H. influenzae and S. pyogenes are not a matter of insufficient power. With four to six cases, the range that brackets the active fractions of S. aureus and S. pneumoniae, the same signal produces a negative Q2 in a fifth to a third of the draws, so the positive results for these two endpoints rest on estimates of very large sampling variability. Since the thinned subsamples retain a more favourable sample-to-variable ratio than ours and describe a diagnosed disease state rather than a structure-activity relationship, these figures are an optimistic bound on what is detectable at such class sizes. Fixing the number of latent variables at two instead of one raises the nested estimates further, to Q2 = 0.45 to 0.58 at p < 0.002, and Route A1u then reproduces the sensitivity, specificity and misclassification count of the original publication exactly (Table S7). It is worth being precise about why this is legitimate. A component number that is fixed a priori is not a source of leakage, and the nested estimate obtained with it is honest; what would be optimistic is to report the maximum over several component numbers as if it had been chosen in advance. Here that distinction can be quantified, because repeating the analysis with the component number selected inside an inner cross-validation loop, which is the doubly nested design used in the original publication, changes the estimates by less than 0.02. The tuning is therefore almost free on this data set, and the two-component model is genuinely the better one. We nevertheless report single-component models for the monoterpenoids throughout, not because tuning is illegitimate but because at n = 31 with three to eleven actives every additional degree of freedom is expensive, whereas 39 nearly balanced samples can afford one."));

push(H2("Why P. aeruginosa is not evaluable"));
push(rich([T("We treat "), I("P. aeruginosa"), T(" as descriptive throughout, and it is worth stating why this is a statistical judgement and not merely a cautious one. With three active compounds among 31, only 4,495 distinct class assignments exist, so the permutation null distribution is coarsely quantised and cannot resolve the observed statistic from its neighbours; leaving one active out removes a third of the active class, so a third of the cross-validation folds fit a fundamentally different model; and the effect-size filter compares three values against 28, where a single compound determines whether a descriptor passes. Two independent observations confirm that what remains is capacity rather than signal. The nested Q\u00b2 rises monotonically with model capacity, from 0.41 with one latent variable through 0.59 with two to 0.73 when the number is chosen freely, which is the behaviour of an artefact being fitted rather than of a signal being found (Table S8). And it falls, rather than rises, when more compounds are held out, from 0.41 under leave-one-out to 0.36 under leave-3-out, with 1.9 % of those folds not modellable at all (Table S9). No standard software warns about any of this, because every individual operation is valid; what fails is the assumption that the estimate is stable, and that is not something a statistical package checks.")]));

push(body("Two further analyses are reported in the Additional file rather than here, because neither changes any conclusion. The first asks whether a second latent variable would help: it does not for Route A, where an inner cross-validation selects a single component for every evaluable endpoint, but it does for the leakage-free Route B, whose honest Q2 rises with three components (Table S8). The second concerns the cross-validated c-statistic, which is identical to the AUC and is known to be biased towards zero for small, imbalanced samples [34]; recomputing it by leave-pair-out shows the bias is present and uniformly conservative, so every AUC reported here is, if anything, too pessimistic (Table S9)."));

push(H2("Why the n:p ratio governs the whole design"));
push(body("All of the above is a consequence of the n:p ratio. At n = 31 the data support only a few effective dimensions, so feeding a large pool into PLS-DA guarantees apparent separation, and even a modest supervised filter can manufacture it. Reducing p, unsupervised wherever possible, and reporting one-component or two-component models keeps the ratio in the range where the cross-validated estimate is meaningful. The rule that p should not exceed n/2, following Ruiz-Perez and colleagues [3], is a useful ceiling, but it is not sufficient on its own, because a 15-descriptor model with a supervised, non-nested selection is still biased. The n:p constraint and honest, nested validation are complementary rather than interchangeable. The exchange of significant endpoints between the full descriptor sets and the 15-descriptor models (Table 1a, Table 3, Table 4) is a further symptom of this instability: when the honest verdict for an endpoint flips with an arbitrary methodological choice, here simply the size of the supervised selection, the apparent signal is not reproducible."));
push(rich([T("It should be said clearly, however, that not everything in these results is unstable, and the stable part is arguably the more important one. Across the four routes compared in Fig. 4a, which differ in whether the reduction is supervised or label-free, in the order of the two reduction steps and in whether the descriptors come from a large computed pool or from a small a priori list, "),
 I("S. aureus"), T(" and "), I("S. pneumoniae"), T(" were significant under every one of them, on both Q\u00b2 and AUC, and "), I("S. pyogenes"),
 T(" gave a negative nested Q\u00b2 under every Route A variant. "), I("H. influenzae"),
 T(" behaved differently between the routes, being non-significant throughout Route A but clearly significant under Route B, and "),
 I("S. pyogenes"), T(" was marginal under Route B, reaching p = 0.035 on Q\u00b2 but not on AUC. Two endpoints are therefore reproducibly associated with the descriptor space and one reproducibly is not, and this agreement is reached by methods that do not share a common supervised selection step. The instability we emphasise is thus a specific one: it concerns which endpoints cross the significance threshold when the size of a supervised selection is varied within a route, not whether any signal exists at all. The distinction matters, because a reader who took away only that small-sample QSAR is unreliable would draw the wrong practical conclusion. The correct conclusion is that a signal can be established even at n = 31, but only by honest validation and preferably by more than one descriptor-selection strategy.")]));
push(body("Older and more conservative QSAR guidance recommends a stricter ratio still, of roughly one descriptor per five compounds, which for n = 31 gives about six descriptors; this rule was developed for ordinary least-squares regression, where chance correlations accumulate quickly as more predictors are added [35]. We tested whether such a stricter ceiling improves the honest result by restricting Route A1 to the six descriptors with the highest effect size per strain and repeating the fully nested permutation test with 2,000 permutations. It did not: for S. aureus the nested Q2 fell to -0.29 (p = 0.327) and for S. pneumoniae to 0.06 (p = 0.061), so both endpoints that were significant with 15 or more descriptors lost significance, while H. influenzae and S. pyogenes remained non-significant as before. Restricting the model to six descriptors therefore discarded genuine signal rather than removing noise. A plausible explanation is that PLS-DA, unlike ordinary least-squares regression, condenses correlated and complementary descriptors into a small number of latent variables and is comparatively robust to a moderate excess of descriptors, so that the stricter n/5 rule, calibrated for regression, is overly conservative here; PLS-DA-specific simulations such as Ruiz-Perez and colleagues instead support a ceiling nearer n/2 [3]. We therefore do not recommend descriptor sets much smaller than about n/2 for PLS-DA models of this kind, and note that the appropriate ceiling may itself depend on the modelling method."));
push(H2("Mechanistic interpretability of the retained descriptors"));
push(body("The descriptors retained by Route B are consistent with the known determinants of monoterpenoid antibacterial activity, namely hydrogen-bonding capacity, molecular size and surface, and lipophilicity, together with functional-group indicators for phenol, carbonyl and alcohol groups. This is in line with the reported membrane-directed mode of action [36]. That a leakage-free, label-independent reduction retains mechanistically plausible descriptors supports the interpretation that the Route B models reflect a real, if moderate, structure-activity relationship rather than a numerical artefact."));
push(H1("Conclusions"));
push(body("Using 31 monoterpenoids against five bacterial strains, we compared the two descriptor-selection routes that dominate small-sample QSAR and examined how they should be validated. Our central finding is methodological. The popular route of computing a very large descriptor pool, reducing it by a supervised effect-size or VIP filter together with correlation grouping, and reporting the cross-validated performance of the resulting model, produces optimistically biased and effectively invalid performance estimates, because the label-based selection is performed outside the cross-validation and the usual permutation test is then circular [7, 8, 34]. Only fully nested validation gives an honest estimate, and under this estimate the apparent ability collapsed for two of the four ostensibly significant strains. Nevertheless, and despite very few and weakly active compounds, a genuine signal survived nesting for two strains, so the supervised route can be worthwhile for some datasets when it is validated honestly. The complementary route, a small and mechanistically chosen interpretable descriptor set reduced only by unsupervised correlation, is leakage-free by construction, recovered a genuine moderate signal for three strains, and is our recommended default for the small-n regime. This recommendation is consistent with the established validation principles for QSAR models, which require an unambiguous algorithm and a defined measure of goodness-of-fit, robustness and predictivity [33, 37]; what our results add is that these requirements are not met by a cross-validated estimate whose descriptor selection lies outside the validation loop."));
push(new Paragraph({ spacing:{before:60,after:60}, children:[new TextRun({text:"Recommendations for descriptor selection under an unfavourable n:p ratio.", bold:true, font:FONT, size:22})]}));
push(body("Synthesising these results, we suggest the following for datasets in which p approaches or exceeds n. First, prefer a small, mechanistically motivated and interpretable descriptor set over an exhaustive data-driven pool, because theory-based pre-selection is subjective but label-independent and therefore leakage-free. Second, reduce redundancy by unsupervised correlation grouping using a Spearman or Pearson threshold of 0.7, and place this step first, before any statistical testing, because it uses no labels, is identical for every endpoint and can be performed on the full dataset without bias. Choosing the representative of each group by an unsupervised criterion such as centrality keeps the entire grouping label-free and is what makes any later count of univariate hits interpretable. Third, keep any supervised selection, whether by effect size, by VIP or by a wrapper method, strictly inside the cross-validation, and never select on labels using all data and then report the cross-validated score of the reduced model [9, 38]. Fourth, report the nested cross-validated Q2 and AUC as the performance estimate and the apparent values only as goodness of fit, and validate significance by permuting the entire pipeline rather than only the final model. Fifth, respect the n:p ceiling, for which p not greater than n/2 is a useful guide, and prefer models with one or two components. Sixth, correct for multiplicity across endpoints and treat all results as hypothesis-generating until they are confirmed on an external test set with a defined applicability domain [2, 39, 40]. Descriptors selected in this way should be described as associated with activity in the dataset, not as validated predictors for new compounds. Following these steps, honest and interpretable structure-activity signals can be extracted even from small and imperfect datasets, while the spurious models that an unfavourable n:p ratio would otherwise produce are avoided."));

// ================= DECLARATIONS =================
push(H1("Declarations"));
